1. e4 {[%eval +0.18] overall lessons from this game: In the modern, the c5 break is almost mandatory, especially against overextended central pawn masses like this. Playing slowly leads to a suffocating position with little counterplay. Often times you may need to sac a pawn temporarily with c5. Embrace the mess, don't aim to control the position and keep it simple at all times, the modern demands sharp c5 breaks and dynamics, so you can't cower from it. When in a worse position, your opponent's job is to seek clarity and convert, your job is to keep pieces on, muddy the waters. Often the best move in worse positions is the least clarifying move, the move that asks the most questions. thirdly, learn to accept positionally worse positions over positions that are completely busted due to an attack. as a positional player this is hard but you must overcome the mental block. } g6 {[%eval +0.55] }  (1... c5  2. Nf3 g6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4)  (1... e5  ) 2. d4 {[%eval +0.48] } Bg7 {[%eval +0.48] } 3. Nc3 {[%eval +0.51] } d6 {[%eval +0.52] } 4. f4 {[%eval +0.35] } a6 {[%eval +0.46] }  (4... Nf6  5. Nf3) 5. a4 {[%eval +0.52] }  (5. Be3  b5 6. a4) Nc6 {[%eval +0.41] } 6. Be3 {[%eval +0.39] } Nf6 {[%eval +0.40] } 7. h3 {[%eval +0.36] } O-O {[%eval +0.43] } 8. e5 {[%eval +0.27] } Nd7 {[%eval +0.48] } 9. Nf3 {[%eval +0.53] } Nb4 {[%eval +0.47] } 10. Bc4 {[%eval +0.50] } e6?! {[%eval +1.01] a bit slow and not in the style of the modern, which is to undermine the dark squares. Better was the immediate c5! I was scared to go for the thematic pawn breaek because I wanted to keep the game controlled, but the whole point of playing a modern is to get a highly dynamic inbalanced game, and it requires precise evaluation of the imbalances. after c5 and e6, black has the inbetween nb6 move! allowing exf7+ but reccouping the pawn after c4. e6 also blocks the development of the light squared bishop, which patrols the weak light squares on that diagonal.}  (10... c5!  11. e6 {[%cal Gd7b6]here i literally stopped calculating at e6. If i had spent more time without discarding the line after c5 I would have found ng6. I needed to expand my candidate moves. after the first move} Nb6 12. exf7+ Kh8 13. Bb3  (13. Bd3  c4 14. Be4 d5 15. Bxg6 hxg6 16. Ng5 Bh6)  (13. Ba2  Nxa2 14. Rxa2 Nc4) c4) 11. O-O {[%eval +0.83] }  (11. Bb3  dxe5 12. dxe5 b6 13. h4 Bb7 14. h5) b6 {[%eval +0.75] } 12. Bb3 {[%eval +0.76] } Bb7 {[%eval +0.72] } 13. Ne2 {[%eval +0.27] } Rc8?! {[%eval +0.69] this is too slow, besst was the immediate c5! The thematic c5 must always be considered in the modern. Here I never really god the powerful pawn break in because I was scared of not being able to win back a pawn. But it's what the position usually demands in this opening, even at the cost of a pawn.}  (13... c5!  14. exd6)  (13... Nd5 {this is also a good alternative, but not as thematic as c5}  14. Bf2 b5! {clamping down on the outpost}) 14. c3 {[%eval +0.38] } Nd5 {[%eval +0.38] } 15. Bf2 {[%eval +0.04] } Bh6?! {[%eval +0.60] Inaccuracy. c5 was best. again, my hesitancy against c5 was probably my biggest positional mistake in this game.}  (15... c5!  16. Qd2  (16. exd6  N7f6 17. c4 Nb4)  (16. Bh4  ) Ne7 17. Bh4 dxe5 18. dxe5 Bxf3 19. gxf3 c4 20. Qd6 Re8 21. Rfd1) 16. Bh4 {[%eval +0.63] } Qe8 {[%eval +0.76] } 17. Qd2 {[%eval +0.42] } dxe5 {[%eval +0.46] } 18. dxe5 {[%eval +0.52] } c5 {[%eval +1.05] } 19. Rae1 {[%eval +0.93] } c4 {[%eval +1.04] } 20. Bc2 {[%eval +0.95] } Nc5 {[%eval +0.90] } 21. Nh2 {[%eval +0.94] } Bg7 {[%eval +0.91] } 22. Ng4 {[%eval +1.10] } Nd7? {[%eval +2.49] This completely misses white can sac the pawn to open lines for the rook and play f5! This intuitively looks very wrong too and completely passive.}  (22... f5  23. exf6 Nxf6 24. Nxf6+ Bxf6 25. Bxf6 Rxf6 {I saw this f5 idea but misevaluated this as much worse for black. This was the least worst option. This positionally looks horrible, but atleast I'm not dealing with a possible checkmating attack like in the game. This was the most resilient path, accepting a positionally terrible position to avoid a much worse attack.} 26. Nd4  (26. Qd4  Qc6 27. Rf2 Nd7) Qd7)  (22... h5  23. Nf6+ Nxf6 24. exf6 Bh6 25. g4 Qc6 26. Ng3 Rfd8)  (22... Qd7  23. Nd4 f5 24. exf6 Nxf6 25. Bxf6 Bxf6 26. Ne5 Qd5 27. Re3 b5 28. Rd1) 23. Nd4 {[%eval +2.15] } h5 {[%eval +2.11] } 24. Nf6+ {[%eval +1.62] }  (24. Nf2  )  (24. Ne3  ) N7xf6 {[%eval +2.03] }  (24... N5xf6  25. exf6  (25. f5  )) 25. exf6?! {[%eval +1.10] Inaccuracy. f5 was best.}  (25. f5  Nh7 26. f6 Rc7 27. Rf2 b5 28. fxg7 Kxg7 29. Ne2 Qc6 30. a5 Rd7) Bh6?? {[%eval +3.10] I saw b5 but thought I had e5 and got impatient when I saw e5. The alternative I saw was taking the pawn and allowing f5 which I thought must have just been losing, But I missed g5 after f5}  (25... Nxf6  26. f5  (26. Bxf6  ) e5! 27. Qg5  (27. fxg6  fxg6 28. Qg5  (28. Bxg6  ) e4 {and black holds on. I completely missed this e5 idea to hold on to my position})  (27. Bg3  Rc5)  (27. Nf3  ) e4 28. Re2  (28. fxg6  fxg6) Rc5 29. Qg3 Re5 30. Bg5 Nd5 31. f6) 26. g4 {[%eval +2.39] } Nxf4? {[%eval +4.11] Mistake. e5 was best.}  (26... e5  27. g5 exd4 28. Rxe8 Rfxe8 29. Bxg6 Bf8 30. Bxh5 Re3 31. Qg2 dxc3 32. bxc3) 27. Rxf4 {[%eval +3.40] } e5?! {[%eval +4.32] Inaccuracy. hxg4 was best.}  (27... hxg4  28. hxg4 Rc5 29. Qh2 e5 30. g5 Qd7 31. Nf5 exf4 32. Nxh6+ Kh8 33. Qxf4) 28. g5 {[%eval +4.24] } Qd7 {[%eval +4.62] } 29. Rxe5 {[%eval +3.98] } Rfe8 {[%eval +4.04] } 30. Nf3 {[%eval +4.19] } Qxh3 {[%eval +4.06] } 31. Qh2?? {[%eval +1.74] Blunder. Be4 was best.}  (31. Be4  Bxe4 32. Rfxe4 Rxe5 33. Nxe5 Bf8 34. Qg2 Qf5 35. Qf3 b5 36. axb5 axb5) Qxh2+ {[%eval +1.83] } 32. Kxh2 {[%eval +1.69] } Bxf3?? {[%eval +4.35] Blunder. Bf8 was best. I did actually see and consider bf8, but thought this forcing line was the best way to try fight for a draw. I completely misevaluated the endgame. Bf8 was much more challenging, keeping the bishop pair with his exposed king, rather than helping him simplify into a winning endgame.}  (32... Bf8 {[%cal Gf8d6]I should have trusted my intuition more, which is my greatest weapon. Here I was calculating bxf3 but my intuition said bf8 was a good practical try, and I was right.}  33. Be4  (33. Rxe8  Rxe8) Bxe4 34. Rfxe4 Bd6 {here, the game remains complex, white will have 2 pieces for a rook but black has decent chances to hold.} 35. Bf2  (35. Bg3  Bxe5 36. Rxe5 Rxe5 37. Bxe5 Rc5 38. Bf4 Ra5) Rcd8 36. Bd4 Bxe5+ 37. Nxe5 Rd5 38. Re3)  (32... Bxg5  ) 33. Rxe8+ {[%eval +4.24] } Rxe8 {[%eval +4.18] } 34. Rxf3 {[%eval +4.28] } Re2+ {[%eval +5.01] } 35. Rf2 {[%eval +4.88] } Rxf2+ {[%eval +4.78] } 36. Bxf2 {[%eval +4.64] } Bxg5 {[%eval +4.63] } 37. Bd4 {[%eval +3.82] This is the line I saw and completely misevaluated from a distance. lesson:; if you're worse, often the best way forward is to avoid clarity at all costs. The line I chose gave my opponent much clarity. I should have kept pieces on the board and posed more practical problems.} b5 {[%eval +3.24] } 38. a5 {[%eval +2.94] } Bf4+ {[%eval +2.95] }  (38... Bc1  )  (38... Bh6  ) 39. Kg2 Bc7 40. Bb6 Be5 41. Be4 g5 42. Bb7 b4 43. cxb4 Bxb2 44. Bxa6 c3 45. Bd3  (45. Bd4  Ba3 46. Bc5  (46. Bd3  )) Ba3 46. Bc5  (46. Bd4  ) g4 47. a6 h4 48. a7 *

